Q: Can I set a global variable using the $GLOBALS array in PHP 8.1.0 and above? A: <p>In PHP version 7, the following code worked for me:</p>
<pre><code>$GLOBALS=array_merge($GLOBALS, $tb);
</code></pre>
<p>In version 8 I got this error:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><code>$GLOBALS</code> can only be modified using the <code>$GLOBALS[$name] = $value</code> syntax</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So I went around it like this:</p>
<pre><code>foreach ($tb as $key =&gt; $value) {
  $GLOBALS[$key]=$value;
}
</code></pre>

Q: Implementing ToSql and FromSql for a composite Postgres type with fields with Diesel A: <p>I found <a href="https://github.com/diesel-rs/diesel/blob/main/examples/postgres/composite_types/README.md" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this example</a> and the <a href="https://docs.rs/diesel/latest/diesel/serialize/trait.WriteTuple.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>WriteTuple</code> documentation</a> which also helped me greatly. I've managed to write the following using Diesel which seems to be working:</p>
<pre class="lang-rs prettyprint-override"><code>type PgUserTuple = (Text, Text);
type PgUser = Record&lt;PgUserTuple&gt;

impl ToSql&lt;schema::sql_types::User, Pg&gt; for User {
    fn to_sql&lt;'b&gt;(
        &amp;'b self,
        out: &amp;mut serialize::Output&lt;'b, '_, Pg&gt;,
    ) -&gt; serialize::Result {
        WriteTuple::&lt;PgUserTuple&gt;::write_tuple(
            &amp;(self.username.clone(), self.password.clone()), &amp;mut out.reborrow()
        )
    }
}

impl FromSql&lt;schema::sql_types::User, Pg&gt; for User {
    fn from_sql(
        bytes: PgValue,
    ) -&gt; deserialize::Result&lt;Self&gt; {
        let (username, password) = FromSql::&lt;PgUser, Pg&gt;::from_sql(bytes)?;
        Ok(Self { username, password })
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><code>Record</code> is the Diesel type corresponding to the tuple SQL type (composite types). <code>WriteTuple</code> is a helper trait for writing <code>ToSql</code> impls for named composite types. I've defined types for converting <code>User</code> into Postgres types (<code>PgUserTuple</code>, <code>PgUser</code>).</p>
